&lt;p&gt;I finally finished my last piece of dissertation work - AI-enabled documentation system featured AR-based smart glasses for EMS in both CO and NYC with my advisor, Dr.
! Thanks for the organization and simulation setup support from
, MD, MPH/MSPH (as well as Nicolena Mitchell and Carl Elston), and
, PhD, DNP, MS, MPH, RN, FSSH, FAAN. Four months after finishing at the Mount Sinai STAR Center, we had a great journey in Denver!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="ideation"&gt;Ideation&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This system was designed around a simple goal: capture patient care process as it happens without taking providers&amp;rsquo; hands or attention away from the patient. The smart-glass application supports medication scanning, contextual photo and video capture, and flexible voice dictation during care. Captured information is then transferred to a connected tablet-based EHR interface for transcription and documentation support.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The AI transforms reviewed dictation into structured field suggestions and an editable patient-care narrative. Clinicians can inspect highlighted evidence, correct extracted values, change the destination EHR field, and choose whether to append, replace, or reject each suggestion. This human-in-the-loop design preserves clinician control while shifting EMS documentation from a delayed, recall-based task toward a real-time capture-and-review workflow.&lt;/p&gt;
